window.__LDNDRC_PAGES = Object.assign(window.__LDNDRC_PAGES || {}, {"tech-stack":"<p>Every component here was chosen for one job. When a layer starts doing two jobs, that is usually where the bug is.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-whole-table\">The whole table</h2><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Layer</th>\n<th>Technology</th>\n<th>The one job</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Orchestration</td>\n<td><span class=\"term\" data-term=\"k3s\" tabindex=\"0\">K3s</span></td>\n<td>Schedule <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">pods</span> on laptops, restart them, hold volumes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Runtime</td>\n<td><span class=\"term\" data-term=\"containerd\" tabindex=\"0\">containerd</span></td>\n<td>Run containers (bundled with <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"k3s\" tabindex=\"0\">K3s</span>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"term\" data-term=\"cni\" tabindex=\"0\">CNI</span></td>\n<td><span class=\"term\" data-term=\"flannel\" tabindex=\"0\">Flannel</span></td>\n<td><span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">Pod</span> networking, deliberately no <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"dds\" tabindex=\"0\">DDS</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"term\" data-term=\"ingress\" tabindex=\"0\">Ingress</span></td>\n<td><span class=\"term\" data-term=\"traefik\" tabindex=\"0\">Traefik</span></td>\n<td>Route four hostnames to the panel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>GPU</td>\n<td>NVIDIA GPU <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"operator\" tabindex=\"0\">Operator</span></td>\n<td>Driver + <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"gpu-timeslicing\" tabindex=\"0\">time-slicing</span> on consumer cards</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Robotics</td>\n<td><span class=\"term\" data-term=\"ros2\" tabindex=\"0\">ROS 2</span> Jazzy</td>\n<td>Per-<span class=\"term\" data-term=\"session\" tabindex=\"0\">session</span> middleware</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Simulation</td>\n<td><span class=\"term\" data-term=\"gazebo\" tabindex=\"0\">Gazebo</span> Harmonic</td>\n<td>Headless physics</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scene stream</td>\n<td><code>gazebo-web</code> + <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"gzweb\" tabindex=\"0\">gzweb</span>/Three.js</td>\n<td>Publish and render the world</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Desktop</td>\n<td>Selkies</td>\n<td>WebRTC Linux desktop</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Editor</td>\n<td><span class=\"term\" data-term=\"code-server\" tabindex=\"0\">code-server</span></td>\n<td>VS Code + terminals in a tab</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"term\" data-term=\"control-plane\" tabindex=\"0\">Control plane</span></td>\n<td>Go (<code>client-go</code>, <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"jwt\" tabindex=\"0\">JWT</span>, <code>net/http</code>)</td>\n<td>Auth, <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"session\" tabindex=\"0\">sessions</span>, onboarding, <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"gateway\" tabindex=\"0\">gateway</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Student UI</td>\n<td>React 18 + Vite + TypeScript</td>\n<td>Login, launch, workspace shell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Persistence</td>\n<td>JSON on a volume</td>\n<td><span class=\"term\" data-term=\"session\" tabindex=\"0\">Sessions</span> and <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"node\" tabindex=\"0\">node</span> approvals</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"the-omissions-are-choices\">The omissions are choices</h2><ul>\n<li><strong>No <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"service\" tabindex=\"0\">service</span> mesh.</strong> Nothing needs mTLS between <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">pods</span>; isolation is a <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"namespace\" tabindex=\"0\">namespace</span> property.</li>\n<li><strong>No external database.</strong> The panel&#39;s state is small, and a database would add a failure mode to a workshop.</li>\n<li><strong>No registry.</strong> Images are imported directly on the demo and pulled from a local registry on a real LAN.</li>\n<li><strong>No cloud identity.</strong> Login must work with the uplink down — see <a href=\"#/lan-first\" class=\"topic-link\">The LAN-first constraint</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"where-the-seams-are\">Where the seams are</h2><p>The Go panel is the only component that talks to <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"kubernetes\" tabindex=\"0\">Kubernetes</span>. If you want to replace the UI, you replace React and keep the API. If you want a different simulator, you keep <code>:9002</code> and swap the bridge. The seams are deliberate, which is why the roadmap can add features without rewrites.</p>\n<h2 id=\"check-it-yourself\">Check it yourself</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">kubectl version --short\nkubectl -n kube-system get pods\nkubectl -n ldndrc get deploy -o wide</code></pre></div>","dev-timeline":"<p>The project was built in seven eras, each one fixing the most painful thing about the previous one. The checkpoints in <code>local_dev_docs/</code> are the receipts.</p>\n<h2 id=\"era-1-one-box-per-student-cp01-cp06\">Era 1 — One box per student (CP01–CP06)</h2><p>Started as scripts and an idea. Ended with a <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"kubernetes\" tabindex=\"0\">Kubernetes</span> <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"deployment\" tabindex=\"0\">Deployment</span>, <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"service\" tabindex=\"0\">Service</span>, and <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pvc\" tabindex=\"0\">PVC</span> per <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"session\" tabindex=\"0\">session</span>, provisioned by a Go panel. The naive version had one shared workspace; the whole point of the era was killing it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"era-2-doors-instead-of-hallways-cp07-cp09\">Era 2 — Doors instead of hallways (CP07–CP09)</h2><p>The first design used path prefixes (<code>/code</code>, <code>/sim</code>, <code>/stream</code>) into a shared room. That broke isolation and made routing ambiguous. The fix was named doors: four hostnames, <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"cookie\" tabindex=\"0\">cookie</span> auth, and a <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"gateway\" tabindex=\"0\">gateway</span> that reads the <code>Host</code> header. Old paths answer <code>404</code> today, on purpose.</p>\n<h2 id=\"era-3-proof-cp08-cp13\">Era 3 — Proof (CP08–CP13)</h2><p>Static manifest checks, then live <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"k3s\" tabindex=\"0\">K3s</span> verification, then GPU-optional scheduling, then deletion of the legacy <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"ingress\" tabindex=\"0\">ingress</span>. Each checkpoint ends with a command whose output is the proof. This is where the habit of &quot;no claim without a verify&quot; started.</p>\n<h2 id=\"era-4-welcoming-laptops-cp14-cp20\">Era 4 — Welcoming laptops (CP14–CP20)</h2><p>The <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"node\" tabindex=\"0\">nodes</span> API, the join binary, <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"mdns\" tabindex=\"0\">mDNS</span> advertisement, the release pipeline, and the first paired rehearsal. Then the real cutover: the platform moved onto the actual master laptop and the demo stood down.</p>\n<h2 id=\"era-5-safety-and-memory-cp21-cp26-cp39-cp40\">Era 5 — Safety and memory (CP21–CP26, CP39–CP40)</h2><p><span class=\"term\" data-term=\"csrf\" tabindex=\"0\">CSRF</span> stamps, <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"session\" tabindex=\"0\">session</span> persistence across small restarts, the student SPA, the instructor console, and a merge that broke login for returning visitors — then the live fix. This era is where most of the hard-won lessons live.</p>\n<h2 id=\"era-6-a-real-robot-world-cp27-cp38\">Era 6 — A real robot world (CP27–CP38)</h2><p>Base image, <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"workspace-image\" tabindex=\"0\">workspace image</span>, immutable tags, disk budget, Selkies 2.0.0, Fuel seeding, live sim proof, compression. The platform stopped using a stand-in and started running actual <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"gazebo\" tabindex=\"0\">Gazebo</span>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"era-7-the-front-door-cp41-cp43\">Era 7 — The front door (CP41–CP43)</h2><p>LAN onboarding portal, durable panel databases, and the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"captive-portal\" tabindex=\"0\">captive portal</span> proven on real hardware. A stranger can now join the Wi-Fi and end up with a working page.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-the-order-teaches\">What the order teaches</h2><p>Every era began with a failure that the previous era&#39;s success exposed. <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"session\" tabindex=\"0\">Sessions</span> were fast, so they needed isolation. Isolation worked, so laptops wanted in. Laptops joined, so the state had to survive redeploys. The <a href=\"#/roadmap\" class=\"topic-link\">What is still left</a> is the next era, written down.</p>\n<h2 id=\"check-it-yourself\">Check it yourself</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\"><span class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls</span> local_dev_docs/checkpoint-*.md | <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc</span> -l\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 43</span></code></pre></div>","roadmap":"<p>The roadmap is written in plain language on purpose. It is the document that keeps the project honest about the difference between &quot;it works on my machine&quot; and &quot;a room of students can use it&quot;.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-status-map\">The status map</h2><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Colour</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Green</td>\n<td>Built and tested on real machines</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Amber</td>\n<td>Half-ready — works partly, needs the next step</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Red</td>\n<td>Missing — real remaining work</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"the-seven-tracks\">The seven tracks</h2><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Track</th>\n<th>State</th>\n<th>What remains</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>R1</strong> Old paths</td>\n<td>done</td>\n<td>nothing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>R2</strong> Memory</td>\n<td>partial</td>\n<td>move <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"session\" tabindex=\"0\">sessions</span> and approvals off <code>emptyDir</code> onto durable storage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>R3</strong> Click safety</td>\n<td>shipped</td>\n<td>login exempted from the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"csrf\" tabindex=\"0\">CSRF</span> check (fixed and verified live)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>R4</strong> Student site</td>\n<td>done</td>\n<td>pretty 3D viewer instead of a raw data frame</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>R5</strong> Welcoming laptops</td>\n<td>partial</td>\n<td>full two-laptop install rehearsal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>R6</strong> Real robot world</td>\n<td>done</td>\n<td>share-faster follow-ups</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>R7 + P5/P6</strong></td>\n<td>left</td>\n<td>encryption, limits, dashboards, Windows path, tidy docs</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"the-next-four-things-in-order\">The next four things, in order</h2><ol>\n<li>Durable state — losing <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"session\" tabindex=\"0\">sessions</span> on a redeploy is the most embarrassing failure left.</li>\n<li>The pretty viewer — the sim streams data, but the browser view is not the final one.</li>\n<li>Two-laptop join — the join path is proven in pieces, not end to end.</li>\n<li>R7 hardening — encryption at rest, resource limits, <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"operator\" tabindex=\"0\">operator</span> dashboards.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"why-this-page-exists\">Why this page exists</h2><p>Because &quot;done&quot; is a claim, and claims decay. Every row here is tied to a checkpoint or a live verification elsewhere in this map. If a row has no proof, it is not done — that is the rule from <a href=\"#/dev-timeline\" class=\"topic-link\">How it was built, in order</a>, applied forward.</p>\n<h2 id=\"check-it-yourself\">Check it yourself</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">git <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">log</span> --oneline -15\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls</span> local_dev_docs/checkpoint-4*.md</code></pre></div><p>Read the last few checkpoints and compare them to the table. Where they disagree, the checkpoints win.</p>","glossary":"<p>Every hard word in this map, in one line each. Come back here whenever a lesson assumes something.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Plain meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Node</strong></td>\n<td>A laptop that is part of the cluster</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Pod</strong></td>\n<td>The smallest unit Kubernetes runs; here, one student&#39;s workspace</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Deployment</strong></td>\n<td>A controller that keeps one pod running and restarts it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Service</strong></td>\n<td>A stable in-cluster address for a pod</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>PVC</strong></td>\n<td>A disk claim; here, the student&#39;s home directory</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ingress</strong></td>\n<td>The front door that reads hostnames and forwards</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Namespace</strong></td>\n<td>A named box for objects; the tenancy boundary</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>RBAC</strong></td>\n<td>Rules about which identity may do which verb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>CNI</strong></td>\n<td>The plugin that gives pods network addresses (Flannel)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>DDS</strong></td>\n<td>ROS 2&#39;s discovery and transport layer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Domain ID</strong></td>\n<td>A number that separates DDS graphs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Control plane</strong></td>\n<td>The panel: auth, sessions, onboarding, gateway</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Host</strong></td>\n<td>A laptop that contributes compute</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Guest</strong></td>\n<td>A laptop that only opens a browser</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Operator</strong></td>\n<td>The person who owns the master</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Session</strong></td>\n<td>One student&#39;s private workspace</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Gateway</strong></td>\n<td>The panel&#39;s reverse proxy into session pods</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>mDNS</strong></td>\n<td>Local network name announcement, no DNS server</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Captive portal</strong></td>\n<td>A page that appears instead of the internet</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"the-one-sentence-to-remember\">The one sentence to remember</h2><p>A student gets a pod; a pod gets a namespace; a namespace makes DDS invisible to everyone else — see <a href=\"#/dds-isolation\" class=\"topic-link\">Why DDS never leaves the pod</a>.</p>","ops-cheatsheet":"<p>The commands an <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"operator\" tabindex=\"0\">operator</span> actually types, in the order they are needed during a <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"session\" tabindex=\"0\">session</span>. This topic is the distilled index; the full table lives in the library.</p>\n<h2 id=\"is-it-up\">Is it up?</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">kubectl -n ldndrc get pods\ncurl -s http://127.0.0.1:8082/healthz</code></pre></div><h2 id=\"read-the-logs\">Read the logs</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">kubectl -n ldndrc logs deploy/ldndrc-control-panel -f\nkubectl -n ldndrc logs &lt;session-pod&gt; -c workspace -f</code></pre></div><h2 id=\"test-the-money-path\">Test the money path</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">curl -s -o /dev/null -w <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;%{http_code}\\n&#x27;</span> \\\n  -H <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Host: editor.ros-platform.local&#x27;</span> -H <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Cookie: <span class=\"hljs-variable\">$COOKIE</span>&quot;</span> \\\n  http://127.0.0.1:8082/</code></pre></div><h2 id=\"if-x-run-y\">If X, run Y</h2><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symptom</th>\n<th>First command</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">Pod</span> <code>Pending</code></td>\n<td><code>kubectl -n ldndrc describe pod &lt;pod&gt;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"term\" data-term=\"gateway\" tabindex=\"0\">Gateway</span> <code>503</code></td>\n<td><code>kubectl -n ldndrc get deploy</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"term\" data-term=\"gateway\" tabindex=\"0\">Gateway</span> <code>502</code></td>\n<td><code>kubectl -n ldndrc get pods</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Editor blank</td>\n<td><code>kubectl -n ldndrc logs &lt;pod&gt; -c workspace</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sim blank</td>\n<td><code>supervisorctl status</code> inside the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">pod</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"term\" data-term=\"node\" tabindex=\"0\">Node</span> never joins</td>\n<td><code>kubectl get nodes</code> and <code>K3S_JOIN_URL</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"the-rule\">The rule</h2><p>Never debug two layers at once. Confirm the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">pod</span>, then the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"service\" tabindex=\"0\">service</span>, then the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"gateway\" tabindex=\"0\">gateway</span>, then the browser — in that order. See <a href=\"#/gateway-proxy\" class=\"topic-link\">The cookie gateway</a> for what each status code means.</p>","interview-qa":"<p>Frequently asked questions about LDNDRC, grouped around the problems, architecture, operations, and trade-offs.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-five-clusters\">The five clusters</h2><ol>\n<li><strong>Why</strong> — the problem, the LAN constraint, the economics of donated compute.</li>\n<li><strong>Architecture</strong> — <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">pods</span>, <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"namespace\" tabindex=\"0\">namespaces</span>, <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"session\" tabindex=\"0\">sessions</span>, <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"gateway\" tabindex=\"0\">gateway</span>, isolation.</li>\n<li><strong><span class=\"term\" data-term=\"kubernetes\" tabindex=\"0\">Kubernetes</span> specifics</strong> — <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"rbac\" tabindex=\"0\">RBAC</span> scope, <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pvc\" tabindex=\"0\">PVCs</span>, probes, rollouts, scheduling.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes</strong> — what breaks, how you would know, what you would do.</li>\n<li><strong>Trade-offs</strong> — what was rejected and why.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it</h2><p>Read the question, answer it out loud in three sentences, then check the source. If you cannot name the failure mode, you do not know the feature — a rule that comes straight from <a href=\"#/dev-timeline\" class=\"topic-link\">How it was built, in order</a>&#39;s verify-everything habit.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-one-question-that-covers-the-most-ground\">The one question that covers the most ground</h2><p><em>&quot;Why can two students run the same ROS topic name without interfering?&quot;</em> A good answer touches the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">pod</span> network <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"namespace\" tabindex=\"0\">namespace</span>, <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"flannel\" tabindex=\"0\">Flannel</span>&#39;s traffic profile, the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"domain-id\" tabindex=\"0\">domain ID</span>, and the fact that teardown removes the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"namespace\" tabindex=\"0\">namespace</span> — that is <a href=\"#/dds-isolation\" class=\"topic-link\">Why DDS never leaves the pod</a> in one breath.</p>","contributing":"<p>Contribution is a first-class route through LDNDRC, not an afterthought. The project is a workshop platform, so a useful contribution makes the next workshop safer, clearer, or easier to reproduce.</p>\n<h2 id=\"start-with-the-smallest-slice\">Start with the smallest slice</h2><p>Read <code>CONTRIBUTING.md</code>, then choose one focused change. Good contributions include a corrected command, a hermetic test, a failure-mode explanation, a manifest improvement, or one independently reviewable feature.</p>\n<p>The project has three laws:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>LAN first: the live workshop must not depend on the public internet.</li>\n<li>Isolation is structural: one student gets one private workspace; <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"dds\" tabindex=\"0\">DDS</span> never crosses the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">pod</span> boundary.</li>\n<li>Least privilege is proved: new permissions need a concrete <code>kubectl auth can-i</code> check.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"choose-the-right-shelf\">Choose the right shelf</h2><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Change</th>\n<th>Home</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Design or <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"operator\" tabindex=\"0\">operator</span> reference</td>\n<td><code>docs/</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Historical proof and milestone</td>\n<td><code>local_dev_docs/</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Guided concept lesson</td>\n<td><code>docs-site/content/topics/</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Runtime behaviour</td>\n<td><code>control-panel/</code>, <code>images/</code>, or <code>manifests/</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Public process</td>\n<td><code>.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/</code> or <code>.github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"open-an-issue-well\">Open an issue well</h2><p>The repository includes templates for bug reports, feature requests, and documentation updates. A useful issue names the environment, gives the exact reproduction, includes evidence, and says what could not be tested. Never paste tokens, private IPs, or credentials.</p>\n<p>A feature request should describe the smallest useful slice, its trade-offs, failure mode, and proof command. A documentation issue should include replacement wording or the corrected command rather than only saying that something feels unclear.</p>\n<h2 id=\"make-a-pull-request\">Make a pull request</h2><p>Use the PR template. Explain what changed and why, paste the verification output, update documentation in the same change, and state known gaps. Keep one idea per PR and use a focused branch such as <code>feat/portal</code>, <code>fix/gateway-status</code>, or <code>docs/contribution-route</code>.</p>\n<p>Useful commit shapes are:</p>\n<div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"text\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">text</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-text\">feat(portal): show approval status before download\nfix(gateway): preserve provisioning status\n docs: add a reproducible join rehearsal</code></pre></div><h2 id=\"verify-before-claiming-done\">Verify before claiming done</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\"><span class=\"hljs-built_in\">cd</span> control-panel &amp;&amp; go <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">test</span> ./...\n./test-join.sh\n./test-joining.sh\n./test-captive-trap.sh\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">cd</span> docs-site &amp;&amp; npm run build</code></pre></div><p>For <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"kubernetes\" tabindex=\"0\">Kubernetes</span> changes, add <code>kubectl apply --dry-run=server -f manifests/</code> and the relevant <code>kubectl auth can-i</code> output. For a user-visible change, include a screenshot or a short before/after. If a second laptop, GPU, or real LAN was unavailable, say that plainly.</p>\n<h2 id=\"add-to-the-map\">Add to the map</h2><p>To improve this lesson or add a contribution topic:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Edit <code>content/topics/&lt;id&gt;.md</code> with the problem, mechanism, failure mode, and a runnable Verify command.</li>\n<li>Add the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"node\" tabindex=\"0\">node</span> and its prerequisites to <code>content/graph.json</code>.</li>\n<li>Use <code>[[topic-id]]</code> links for related concepts.</li>\n<li>Run <code>npm run build</code> and check the route in the map.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The contribution templates are available in the Library alongside <code>CONTRIBUTING.md</code>, so the process can be learned and followed from the same site.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-will-not-be-merged\">What will not be merged</h2><p>No committed secrets, unexplained cluster-wide permissions, shared workspaces, cloud-only live paths, silent driver installation, default auto-approval, copied nixmap assets, or “done” claims without a test, command, checkpoint, or live proof.</p>\n<h2 id=\"verify-it-yourself\">Verify it yourself</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\"><span class=\"hljs-built_in\">test</span> -f CONTRIBUTING.md &amp;&amp; \\\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">test</span> -f .github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md &amp;&amp; \\\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">test</span> -f .github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/bug_report.md &amp;&amp; \\\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;contribution workflow is present&quot;</span></code></pre></div>"});
